      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "**Yes.** The relevant component-issue event was created **75 minutes after** the September 2025 cost period entered Pending Close, yet its recorded accounting status is **`POSTED`**.\n\n| Audit item | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Manufacturing order | `MO-25-09-1070` |\n| Work order ID | `WO-2025-09-004` — Cooler Plate Pack, Racine |\n| Source transaction | `TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070` |\n| Cost event | `CE-250904-0004` — `INPUT` (component issue) |\n| Amount | **USD $284,763.42** — 28,476,342 cents |\n| Event-created time | **`2025-09-30T19:15:00`** |\n| September Pending Close time | **`2025-09-30T18:00:00`** |\n| Time after Pending Close | **75 minutes** — 4,500 seconds, or 1 hour 15 minutes |\n| Recorded accounting status | **`POSTED`** |\n| Consistent with documented treatment? | **No** |\n\n### Policy conclusion\nOracle’s supplied **Cost Management 26B** documentation states that newly created transactions falling in the current or an earlier period **will not be staged or processed after the period enters Pending Close**; processing resumes after the period is closed or reopened. Revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments are exceptions, but this **component-issue `INPUT` event is not one of them**. Its recorded `POSTED` status is therefore inconsistent with that documented treatment.\n\n**Sources:** `work_orders`, `cost_periods`, and `work_order_cost_events` in the supplied `system/oracle_scm_cost_management/state.db` records; *Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting*, Oracle 26B guide, printed page 225 / PDF page 233.\n\nTimestamps are reproduced exactly as stored; the records do not specify a time zone. The review was read-only, and **no database records were changed**. The verified report and provenance were saved to `/cache/work/worker_85c0bb4450b2/audit-report.json`.",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": []
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer"
    }
